Chapter: RocFoldProduct
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Ported from roc-lang/roc, src/eval/test/eval_closure_recursion_tests.zig,
 "inspect: inline fold product lambda":

     (|list, init, step| {
         var $state = init
         for item in list {
             $state = step($state, item)
         }
         $state
     })([2, 3, 4], 1, |acc, x| acc * x)

 and Roc expects 24.

 The adaptations are `roc-fold-sum`'s, unchanged: Roc's decimal default
 spells its answer `24.0`, and Roc's `var` loop becomes a named recursive
 definition because that is Codex's only loop.

 **This differs from `roc-fold-sum` in one operator and one list, and it is
 here anyway.** The suite has it as a separate case, and the borrowed suite
 is worth more when it is taken as given than when it is filtered through
 our guess about which of its cases are redundant. What it does add on our
 side: the step closure is entered through a different builtin, and this
 emitter maps every arithmetic builtin separately.

Section: Functions

  fold-loop : List Integer, Integer, (Integer, Integer -> Integer), Integer -> Integer
  fold-loop (xs) (acc) (step) (i) =
   if i >= list-length xs then acc
   else fold-loop xs (step acc (list-at xs i)) step (i + 1)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   let total = (\xs base step -> fold-loop xs base step 0) [2, 3, 4] 1 (\acc x -> acc * x)
   in print-line-uni (show total)
  end
